= Sociology of knowledge =

The '''sociology of knowledge''' studies relationships between knowledge, belief, thought, and the social conditions in which they are produced, maintained, distributed, contested, and changed. It asks how institutions, social positions, professional communities, historical circumstances, language, authority, and everyday practices influence what people treat as knowledge.

The field is descriptive and explanatory rather than a shortcut for deciding whether a claim is true. Showing that a belief has social causes does not by itself prove the belief false, and showing that a belief is widely institutionalized does not prove it true. The sociology of knowledge therefore intersects with [[Epistemology]] while remaining methodologically distinct from normative questions about justification.<ref name="social-epistemology">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology-social/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Social Epistemology.”]</ref>

== Historical origins ==

The field developed from several intellectual traditions rather than from one founding theory.

Émile Durkheim and Marcel Mauss examined how classification systems and conceptual categories are related to forms of social organization. Their 1903 work on classification and Durkheim's later sociology of religion helped establish the idea that categories of thought can have social histories.

Max Scheler used the term sociology of knowledge in the 1920s and examined relations between social conditions and forms of thought.

Karl Mannheim made the field widely influential through work on ideology, social location, and the historical conditioning of thought, especially ''Ideology and Utopia''.<ref name="mannheim">[https://www.routledge.com/Ideology-and-Utopia-An-Introduction-to-the-Sociology-of-Knowledge/Mannheim/p/book/9780415060547 Mannheim, K., ''Ideology and Utopia: An Introduction to the Sociology of Knowledge'', later English editions.]</ref>

Robert K. Merton systematized and critically reviewed the field in American sociology and later contributed to the sociology of science.

Peter L. Berger and Thomas Luckmann broadened the program in ''The Social Construction of Reality'' (1966), emphasizing everyday knowledge, institutionalization, legitimation, and socialization rather than restricting analysis to intellectual or ideological systems.<ref name="berger">[https://books.google.com/books/about/The_Social_Construction_of_Reality.html?id=rGg9mT_JNIEC Berger, P. L. and Luckmann, T. (1966), ''The Social Construction of Reality: A Treatise in the Sociology of Knowledge''.]</ref>

== Social location and thought ==

A basic question is whether social position influences what problems people notice, which concepts they use, and which explanations appear plausible.

Relevant dimensions can include:

* class;
* occupation;
* profession;
* institution;
* political position;
* religion;
* generation;
* education;
* historical period.

The claim is not that every belief can be predicted from social identity. Rather, social location can shape access to experiences, information, incentives, vocabulary, and interpretive traditions.

== Ideology ==

Mannheim's work made ideology central to the sociology of knowledge.

A narrow concept of ideology treats particular claims as distortions serving interests. A broader sociology of knowledge examines how entire styles of thought can be historically and socially situated.

This creates a reflexive problem: if all thought is socially situated, sociological analysis is socially situated too.

Mannheim attempted to address this without reducing every viewpoint to arbitrary relativism. Later scholarship continues to debate the relationship between social determination, perspectivism, and objectivity.

== Social construction of reality ==

Berger and Luckmann analyze how recurring human activity becomes habitualized and institutionalized.

A simplified sequence is:

<pre>
repeated action
→ habitualization
→ institutionalization
→ legitimation
→ socialization
→ taken-for-granted reality
</pre>

Institutions become experienced as objective social facts even though they are historically produced and reproduced through human activity.

This does not mean that all physical reality is created by belief. Social construction claims must specify what is socially produced: classifications, roles, institutions, conventions, identities, standards, or interpretations.

== Everyday knowledge ==

Berger and Luckmann deliberately expanded the field beyond elite intellectual systems.

Everyday life depends on shared stocks of knowledge:

* how to use institutions;
* what roles imply;
* which behaviors are normal;
* how language categorizes situations;
* which authorities are trusted.

Much of this knowledge is practical and taken for granted rather than explicitly theorized.

The sociology of knowledge therefore studies ordinary social reality as well as philosophy, science, or ideology.

== Institutions and legitimation ==

Institutions stabilize expectations.

A court, laboratory, school, religious organization, or professional association can define procedures for producing and recognizing valid claims.

Legitimation explains and justifies institutional arrangements through narratives, doctrines, rules, credentials, or symbolic systems.

Authority can improve coordination and preserve expertise, but it can also reproduce error or exclusion.

== Language and classification ==

Language provides categories through which experience is described and communicated.

Classification systems can shape what becomes administratively or scientifically visible.

Examples include:

* medical diagnostic categories;
* legal classifications;
* occupational categories;
* scientific taxonomies;
* educational credentials.

Sociologists can study how categories emerge, who controls them, and what consequences follow.

A classification can be socially produced and still track real differences. Social origin and empirical adequacy are separate questions.

== Sociology of scientific knowledge ==

The '''sociology of scientific knowledge''' (SSK) applies sociological analysis to scientific belief, practice, and institutions.

Research topics include:

* laboratories;
* peer review;
* scientific controversy;
* instrumentation;
* disciplinary boundaries;
* citation networks;
* professional incentives;
* consensus formation.

SSK challenged the idea that sociology should explain only scientific error while treating accepted truth as socially uninteresting.

Strong versions of this program have generated controversy because symmetrical sociological explanation can appear to neglect differences in evidential support. A careful account can study social processes without treating evidence as irrelevant.

== Sociology of scientific ignorance ==

Related research studies how ignorance is produced, maintained, distributed, or strategically amplified.

Ignorance can result from:

* unavailable evidence;
* uncertainty;
* institutional neglect;
* secrecy;
* deliberate misinformation;
* research priorities;
* methodological limits.

The study of ignorance complements the study of socially organized knowledge.

== Expertise and authority ==

Modern societies depend on expert testimony.

Sociological questions include:

* how expert status is created;
* how credentials function;
* how professions regulate membership;
* how laypeople assess specialists;
* how experts communicate uncertainty;
* how institutions handle disagreement.

Expert authority can be rationally justified while remaining fallible.

Trust should be connected to demonstrated methods, accountability, and evidence rather than status alone.

== Knowledge and power ==

Knowledge institutions distribute authority unequally.

Governments, universities, publishers, platforms, professions, and firms can influence:

* which questions receive resources;
* who gains access;
* what is archived;
* which classifications become official;
* whose testimony is treated as credible.

Michel Foucault's historical analyses of discourse, institutions, and knowledge–power relations strongly influenced adjacent scholarship, although his genealogy and archaeology are not simply identical to classical sociology of knowledge.

== Methods ==

Methods include:

* historical analysis;
* archival research;
* ethnography;
* interviews;
* participant observation;
* discourse analysis;
* document analysis;
* citation and bibliometric analysis;
* network analysis;
* comparative institutional study.

Method should follow the question.

A citation network can show patterns of reference but does not by itself establish intellectual influence. An interview can reveal participants' accounts but not necessarily the full causal history of an institution.

== Reflexivity ==

The sociology of knowledge is reflexive because its own theories are produced in social contexts.

Researchers therefore need to examine:

* their categories;
* institutional position;
* incentives;
* data access;
* interpretive assumptions.

Reflexivity does not require abandoning factual inquiry. It requires making conditions of inquiry visible.

== Relation to social epistemology ==

[[Epistemology]] asks normative questions about knowledge and justification.

Social epistemology studies how testimony, institutions, disagreement, expertise, and group processes affect justified belief.<ref name="social-epistemology" />

The sociology of knowledge is more directly concerned with empirical and historical explanation of how knowledge claims and knowledge institutions develop.

The fields overlap but have different primary questions.

== Relation to knowledge ecosystems ==

A [[Knowledge ecosystem]] is a systems framework for actors, resources, infrastructure, and knowledge flows.

The sociology of knowledge can analyze the social structures, authority relations, norms, and institutional histories inside such an ecosystem.

== Digital knowledge environments ==

Search engines, social platforms, online encyclopedias, preprint servers, and AI systems change how knowledge is produced and distributed.

Sociological analysis can examine:

* ranking power;
* platform moderation;
* algorithmic visibility;
* credential changes;
* automated content production;
* unequal access;
* collective correction.

A computational system can become part of the social organization of knowledge without itself settling the truth of the content it distributes.

== Epistemic boundary ==

The sociology of knowledge explains conditions under which beliefs and knowledge claims arise and gain authority.

It does not license the genetic fallacy: evaluating a claim solely by its social origin.

A complete analysis should keep separate:

# the social explanation of a belief;
# the evidence supporting the belief;
# the normative standards used to call it knowledge.

